WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.295 --> 00:00:17.320
Zelenskyy was, in Ukraine, easily the best
recognizable comedian

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.480
and the most successful one.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.209
And the project that was his flagship thing,
was a regular comedy show.

00:00:23.209 --> 00:00:26.210
It was not what you would call highbrow humor.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:30.640
There was quite a lot of kind of 
primitive humor, I would say.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.548
And quite a lot of sophisticated 
making fun of politics.

00:00:34.548 --> 00:00:37.960
He would just do these really funny and clever
takedowns of

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.277
Ukrainian politicians, Russian politicians,
you know,

00:00:40.277 --> 00:00:43.525
because it is in many ways, a shared 
cultural and political space.

00:01:01.709 --> 00:01:04.220
Servant of the People is a good show.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.000
It's really funny.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.460
It’s about a history teacher who's sort of
thrust into the presidency

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.950
because of a video of him that goes viral.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:16.320
This is your ultimate, like a regular person 
suddenly gets power all over the country.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.999
And his heart in the right place 
but everything is against him.

00:01:18.999 --> 00:01:20.359
There's corruption everywhere.

00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:22.869
And he's — he just wants to change the country.

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:27.861
He almost, like, showed on the screen
what kind of president he would be.

00:01:27.861 --> 00:01:32.153
And I think that was his basically 
campaign manifesto.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:53.399
He's basically asking people to submit the
five main problems that they see in Ukraine.

00:01:53.399 --> 00:01:56.559
You could call it like a crowdsourced 
political program.

00:01:56.559 --> 00:01:59.549
His whole campaign was a number of videos
like that, where

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:03.061
instead of doing a professional video which
he easily could have done

00:02:03.061 --> 00:02:05.330
he was doing this little selfie clips.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:08.531
It works really well for his image of 
nontraditional, anti-establishment guy.

00:02:08.531 --> 00:02:13.832
He was keeping his program 
pretty vague and empty.

00:02:13.832 --> 00:02:15.713
And it worked.

00:02:42.196 --> 00:02:46.627
His victory speech — he said, I am, the
hope for change

00:02:46.627 --> 00:02:50.833
not just for people in Ukraine 
but for all people

00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:52.876
hinting obviously on Russia.

00:02:52.876 --> 00:02:56.166
I still think that Putin holds 
a big grudge on this.

00:02:56.166 --> 00:02:57.721
Nobody knows what Putin thought.

00:02:57.721 --> 00:03:02.045
Probably in all likelihood the leadership
of Russia was thinking that he’s inexperienced,

00:03:02.045 --> 00:03:03.210
he’s new.

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.514
This is our chance.

00:03:04.514 --> 00:03:08.973
It's my hypothesis that he was 
looking at Zelenskyy

00:03:08.973 --> 00:03:12.917
as a very easy partner, who can be played.

00:03:12.917 --> 00:03:17.166
It's eerie to me to watch it now, to be honest.

00:03:20.106 --> 00:03:23.877
"I think that there's a high chance 
of ending the war."

00:03:23.877 --> 00:03:25.013
"When?"

00:03:30.606 --> 00:03:31.662
"When?"

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.023
"As soon as possible."

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.585
"I want to believe that it will be this year."

00:03:47.585 --> 00:03:53.906
The presidency was defined by a pretty consistent
and precipitous decline in popularity.

00:03:53.906 --> 00:03:58.631
We're talking about ratings of 30s 
even down to like 20s.

00:03:58.631 --> 00:04:01.157
It was connected to several issues.

00:04:01.157 --> 00:04:03.465
There was no progress in peace.

00:04:03.465 --> 00:04:09.488
There were implications of his company 
listed in the Pandora Papers.

00:04:09.488 --> 00:04:13.827
He was trying to accumulate more power in
the office of the president

00:04:13.827 --> 00:04:15.622
which Ukrainians despise.

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:18.574
He started cracking down on opposition.

00:04:18.574 --> 00:04:21.605
He was changing tremendously and
he was becoming a strongman.

00:04:21.605 --> 00:04:26.925
People saw him slipping towards the usual
way of doing business in Ukraine.

00:04:26.925 --> 00:04:29.940
A lot of Ukrainians were not seeing it as
a successful presidency.

00:04:47.585 --> 00:04:50.476
What is happening in that clip is that he
is holding a press conference 

00:04:50.476 --> 00:04:51.476
for foreign media.

00:04:51.476 --> 00:04:56.022
Zelenskyy was trying to downplay the threat,
but also putting out some confusing messages.

00:04:56.022 --> 00:04:59.188
These expectations of the war

00:04:59.188 --> 00:05:03.743
they forced many investors 
to leave Ukraine.

00:05:03.743 --> 00:05:07.140
And, of course, for President Zelenskyy it
was unacceptable.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.942
In the weeks that led up to the crisis, from
the communication point of view

00:05:10.942 --> 00:05:12.915
I don't think that he did a very good job.

00:05:31.735 --> 00:05:36.955
At the outbreak of war, there was fear and
there was a suspicion that he may

00:05:36.955 --> 00:05:40.860
you know, capitulate, he will concede, 
he'll get scared.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.180
That clip was perceived that the captain 
is not abandoning the ship.

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.770
From that moment, people understood 
he's not surrendering.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.238
And there are millions of Ukrainians 
who are ready to put up that fight.

00:05:52.238 --> 00:05:54.178
Yes, yes, it definitely was a turning point.

00:05:54.178 --> 00:05:56.823
If you watch a clip of Zelenskyy talking,
for example

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:59.561
 to the Western press in January again

00:05:59.561 --> 00:06:03.235
and this clip of Zelenskyy standing 
in the street of Kyiv

00:06:03.235 --> 00:06:05.175
you can think that these are 
two different people.

00:06:05.175 --> 00:06:08.684
But if you compare this clip 
with Zelenskyy of 2019

00:06:08.684 --> 00:06:10.455
when he was campaigning for president

00:06:10.455 --> 00:06:11.675
that's the same person.

00:06:11.675 --> 00:06:16.955
In terms of style, in terms of how, you know,
sincere and open he is

00:06:16.955 --> 00:06:18.367
it's like we made a full circle.

00:06:18.367 --> 00:06:22.302
We came back to the 2019 Zelenskyy, 
which is the guy people voted for.

00:06:39.874 --> 00:06:42.424
His speech to the European Parliament.

00:06:42.424 --> 00:06:43.439
It was really striking.

00:06:43.439 --> 00:06:47.679
As it was translated for the members of the
European Parliament you hear the translator

00:06:47.679 --> 00:06:52.109
fighting back tears as he's 
translating Zelenskyy's words.

00:06:52.109 --> 00:06:54.641
I've never heard that happen.

00:06:54.641 --> 00:06:56.973
"We are fighting."

00:06:56.973 --> 00:07:01.435
"Just for our land and for our freedom...

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:08.664
despite the fact that all our citizens
of our country are now blocked..."

00:07:08.664 --> 00:07:11.072
The moment was so emotional and raw.

00:07:11.072 --> 00:07:14.307
I think it really helped to change 
a lot of minds in Europe.

00:07:14.307 --> 00:07:18.944
They have to understand that for Ukraine,
it's their battle for existence.

00:07:18.944 --> 00:07:24.764
His message is also if we stop Putin at
the Ukrainian border, everybody will be safer.

00:07:24.764 --> 00:07:28.605
His appeals to Europe, have been 
very much impactful

00:07:28.605 --> 00:07:33.053
and really making it morally and politically
impossible for them

00:07:33.053 --> 00:07:36.104
to stay on the sidelines and not support Ukraine.

00:07:36.104 --> 00:07:38.713
Today, his background as somebody who

00:07:38.713 --> 00:07:41.304
was working with the audience his whole life
is coming in handy.

00:07:41.304 --> 00:07:45.447
He definitely knows how to command the audience,
how to grasp their attention

00:07:45.447 --> 00:07:46.995
how to how to put on a show, so to say.

00:07:46.995 --> 00:07:50.392
He probably wasn't the best president in peacetime.

00:07:50.392 --> 00:07:56.494
But he was very good in the beginning of the war,
creating hope, uniting the nation

00:07:56.494 --> 00:07:59.604
and giving an impetus for resistance.

00:07:59.604 --> 00:08:01.144
He is not the president anymore.

00:08:01.144 --> 00:08:02.955
He's the leader of the nation.

00:08:02.955 --> 00:08:07.115
I know because I asked people in Ukraine,
they find it convincing.

00:08:07.115 --> 00:08:09.773
To stay in the capital, in Kyiv

00:08:09.773 --> 00:08:14.249
that's really inspiring for a lot of
people in the country to see.

00:08:14.249 --> 00:08:17.026
I think it shouldn't be forgotten that

00:08:17.026 --> 00:08:20.299
there are thousands of regular Ukrainians
who are demonstrating 

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:22.115
even bigger bravery every day.

00:08:22.115 --> 00:08:26.146
Unarmed civilians trying to stop tanks 
with their bare hands.

00:08:26.146 --> 00:08:30.869
This spirit of regular Ukrainians is 
what's inspiring Zelenskyy too.

00:08:30.869 --> 00:08:34.120
He's proven worthy of the nation he’s leading.

